===Etymology===
{{unc|en}}. See {{w|Hoosier|Wikipedia's article on the subject}} for theories. Popularized by the 1830 {{w|John Finley}} poem “The Hoosier’s Nest”.<ref name="wapo">{{cite-web |en |title=‘Hoosier’ is now the official name for Indiana folk. But what does it even mean? |titleurl=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/morning-mix/wp/2017/01/13/hoosier-is-now-the-official-name-for-indiana-folk-but-what-does-it-even-mean/ |last=Mettler |first=Katie |date=2017-01-13 |accessdate=2022-12-19 |work={{w|Washington Post}}}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhuʒɚ/|a=GenAm}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A native or resident of the U.S. state of [[Indiana]].
#: {{syn|en|Indianan<qq:exonym, often proscribed>}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author= Colin Woodard|chapter=16|title= American nations|edition=|location=New York|publisher=Penguin|year=2011|isbn= 978-1-101-54445-7|page=|text= Indiana’s Borderlanders called themselves '''Hoosiers''', came from the backcountry of Kentucky and western Virginia, and were ambivalent about slavery.}}
# Someone associated with {{w|Indiana University}}, for example as a student, alum, or sports team member, or as a fan. This is also the university's sports [[mascot]].
# {{senseid|en|boorish, contemptible white person}} {{lb|en|slang|St. Louis|Missouri}} An [[uneducated]], [[tasteless]], [[boorish]] [[white]] person.
#: {{syn|en|white trash}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|hoser<id:boorish, contemptible person><q:Canada>}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2014|author=Emily Giffin|title=The Emily Giffin Collection: Volume 2: Baby Proof|isbn=1466882999|passage="The mall? Belinda, mall pickups are for '''hoosiers'''," I say, St. Louis slang for white trash. "With femullets." [...] "But Jake's no '''hoosier'''."}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2015|author=Pate McMichael|title=Klandestine: How a Klan Lawyer and a Checkbook Journalist ...|page=2|passage=Taken as a whole, the article portrayed Ray as an indigent, racist “'''Hoosier'''” (St. Louis slang for redneck) with an inept, habitual tendency to commit petty crimes.}}
# {{lb|en|US|historical}} A kind of [[cupboard]] or [[dresser]] with shelves, drawers, etc.; a [[kitchenet]].

